.TH "rofi-theme" "5" "" "rofi" "File Formats Manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
\f[B]rofi-theme\f[R] - Rofi theme format files
.SH Getting started with theming
.PP
The easiest way to get started theming rofi is by modifying your
existing theme.
.PP
Themes can be modified/tweaked by adding theming elements to the end of
the
.PD 0
.P
.PD
config file.
The default location of this file is
\f[B]\[ti]/.config/rofi/config.rasi\f[R], if the file does not exists,
you can create it.
.PP
A basic config:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
configuration {
  modes: [ combi ];
  combi-modes: [ window, drun, run ];
}

\[at]theme \[dq]gruvbox-light\[dq]
 
/* Insert theme modifications after this */
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
For example if we want to change the \f[B]Type to filter\f[R] text in
the entry box we append the following:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
entry {
    placeholder: \[dq]Type here\[dq];
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
In the above section, \f[B]entry\f[R] indicates the widget,
\f[B]placeholder\f[R] is the property we want to modify and we set it to
the string \f[B]\[dq]Type here\[dq]\f[R].
To find the commonly available widgets in rofi, see the `Basic
structure' section.
.PP
To change the mouse over cursor to a pointer, add:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
entry {
    placeholder: \[dq]Type here\[dq];
    cursor: pointer;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
For the next modification, we want to add the icon after each text
element and increase the size.
First we start by modifying the \f[B]element\f[R] widget:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]

element {
  orientation: horizontal;
  children: [ element-text, element-icon ];
  spacing: 5px;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Resulting in the following packing:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ 
│ element                                                             │ 
│ ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────┐ │ 
│ │element─text                                 │ │ element─icon    │ │ 
│ └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘ └─────────────────┘ │ 
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ 
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The \f[B]element\f[R] (container) widget hold each entry in the
\f[B]listview\f[R], we add the two pre-defined children in the order we
want to show.
We also specify the packing direction (\f[B]orientation\f[R]) and the
spacing between the children (\f[B]spacing\f[R]).
We specify the space between the two children in absolute pixels
(\f[B]px\f[R]).
.PP
To increase the icon-size, we need to modify the \f[B]element-icon\f[R]
widget.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
element-icon {
    size: 2.5em;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ 
│ element                                                             │ 
│ ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────┐ │ 
│ │element─text                                 │ │    element      │ │ 
│ │                                             │ │       ─         │ │ 
│ │                                             │ │     icon        │ │ 
│ └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘ └─────────────────┘ │ 
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ 
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
In this example we specify the size in the
em (https://www.w3.org/Style/LieBos3e/em) unit.
.PP
Now lets change the text color of both the \f[B]entry\f[R] and the
\f[B]element-text\f[R] widget to red and background to blue.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
entry, element-text {
  text-color: red;
  background-color: rgb(0,0,255);
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Here we use two different methods of writing down the color, for
\f[B]text-color\f[R] we used a named color, for
\f[B]background-color\f[R] we specify it in \f[B]rgb\f[R].
We also specify the property for multiple widgets by passing a comma
separated list of widget names.
.PP
If you want to center the text relative to the icon, we can set this:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
element-text {
    vertical-align: 0.5;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ 
│ element                                                             │ 
│ ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────┐ │ 
│ │                                             │ │    element      │ │ 
│ │element-text                                 │ │       ─         │ │ 
│ │                                             │ │     icon        │ │ 
│ └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘ └─────────────────┘ │ 
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ 
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
We can also specify the color and width of the cursor.
You could, for example, create a crimson block cursor like this:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
entry {
  cursor-color: rgb(220,20,60);
  cursor-width: 8px;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
By default, the \f[B]cursor-color\f[R] will be the same as the
\f[B]text-color\f[R].
The \f[B]cursor-width\f[R] will always default to 2 pixels.
.PP
If you want to see the complete theme, including the modification you
can run:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
rofi -dump-theme
\f[R]
.fi
.SH Default theme loading
.PP
By default, rofi loads the default theme.
This theme is \f[B]always\f[R] loaded.
The default configuration contains:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
\[at]theme \[dq]default\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To unload the default theme, and load another theme, add the
\f[B]\[at]theme\f[R] statement to your \f[B]config.rasi\f[R] file.
.PP
If you have a theme loaded via \f[B]\[at]theme\f[R] or use the default
theme, you can tweak it by adding overriding elements at the end of your
\f[B]config.rasi\f[R] file.
.PP
For the difference between \f[B]\[at]import\f[R] and
\f[B]\[at]theme\f[R] see the \f[B]Multiple file handling\f[R] section in
this manpage.
.PP
To see the default theme, run the following command:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
rofi -no-config -dump-theme
\f[R]
.fi
.SH Description
.PP
The need for a new theme format was motivated by the fact that the way
rofi handled widgets has changed.
From a very static drawing of lines and text to a nice structured form
of packing widgets.
This change made it possible to provide a more flexible theme framework.
The old theme format and config file are not flexible enough to expose
these options in a user-friendly way.
Therefore, a new file format has been created, replacing the old one.
.SH Format specification
.SH Encoding
.PP
The encoding of the file is UTF-8.
Both unix (\f[B]\[rs]n\f[R]) and windows (\f[B]\[rs]r\[rs]n\f[R])
newlines format are supported.
But unix is preferred.
.SH Comments
.PP
C and C++ file comments are supported.
.IP \[bu] 2
Anything after \f[B]//\f[R] and before a newline is considered a
comment.
.IP \[bu] 2
Everything between \f[B]/*\f[R] and \f[B]*/\f[R] is a comment, this
comment can span multiple lines.
.PP
Comments can be nested and the C comments can be inline.
.PP
The following is valid:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
// Magic comment.
property: /* comment */ value;
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
However, this is not:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
prop/*comment*/erty: value;
\f[R]
.fi
.SH White space
.PP
White space and newlines, like comments, are ignored by the parser.
.PP
This:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
property: name;
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Is identical to:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
     property             :
name

;
\f[R]
.fi
.SH File extension
.PP
The preferred file extension for the new theme format is \f[B]rasi\f[R].
This is an abbreviation for \f[B]r\f[R]ofi \f[B]a\f[R]dvanced
\f[B]s\f[R]tyle \f[B]i\f[R]nformation.
If a theme file is split over multiple files, include files can have
the: \f[B]rasinc\f[R] extension.
.SH Basic Structure
.PP
Each element has a section with defined properties.
Global properties can be defined in section \f[B]* { }\f[R].
Sub-section names begin with an optional hash symbol \f[B]#\f[R].
.PP
It is advised to define the \f[I]global properties section\f[R] on top
of the file to make inheritance of properties clearer.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
/* Global properties section */
* {
    // list of properties
}

/* Element theme section. */
{element path} {
    // list of properties
}
{elements... } {
    // list of properties
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If there are multiple sections with the same name, they are merged.
Duplicate properties are overwritten and the last parsed entry kept.
.SH Global properties section
.PP
A theme can have one or more global properties sections.
If there is more than one, they will be merged.
.PP
The global properties section denotes the defaults for each element.
Each property of this section can be referenced with
\f[B]\[at]{identifier}\f[R] (See Properties section)
.PP
A global properties section is indicated with a \f[B]*\f[R] as element
path.
.SH Element theme section
.PP
A theme can have multiple element theme sections.
.PP
The element path can consist of multiple names separated by whitespace
or dots.
Each element may contain any number of letters, numbers and
\f[B]-\f[R]\[cq]s.
The first element in the element path can optionally start with a
\f[B]#\f[R] (for historic reasons).
Multiple elements can be specified by a \f[B],\f[R].
.PP
This is a valid element name:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
element normal.normal {
    background-color: blue;
}
button {
    background-color: blue;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
And is identical to:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
element normal normal, button {
    background-color: blue;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Each section inherits the global properties.
Properties can be explicitly inherited from their parent with the
\f[B]inherit\f[R] keyword.
In the following example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
window {
 a: 1;
 b: 2;
 children: [ mainbox ];
}
mainbox {
    a: inherit;
    b: 4;
    c: 8;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The element \f[B]mainbox\f[R] will have the following set of properties
(if \f[B]mainbox\f[R] is a child of \f[B]window\f[R]):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
a: 1;
b: 4;
c: 8;
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If multiple sections are defined with the same name, they are merged by
the parser.
If multiple properties with the same name are defined in one section,
the last encountered property is used.
.SH Properties Format
.PP
The properties in a section consist of:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
{identifier}: {value};
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Both fields are mandatory for a property.
.PP
The \f[B]identifier\f[R] names the specified property.
Identifiers can consist of any combination of numbers, letters and `-'.
It must not contain any whitespace.
The structure of the \f[B]value\f[R] defines the type of the property.
The current parser does not define or enforce a certain type of a
particular \f[B]identifier\f[R].
When used, values with the wrong type that cannot be converted are
ignored.
.PP
The current theme format supports different types:
.IP \[bu] 2
a string
.IP \[bu] 2
an integer number
.IP \[bu] 2
a fractional number
.IP \[bu] 2
a boolean value
.IP \[bu] 2
a color
.IP \[bu] 2
image
.IP \[bu] 2
text style
.IP \[bu] 2
line style
.IP \[bu] 2
a distance
.IP \[bu] 2
a padding
.IP \[bu] 2
a border
.IP \[bu] 2
a position
.IP \[bu] 2
a reference
.IP \[bu] 2
an orientation
.IP \[bu] 2
a cursor
.IP \[bu] 2
a list of keywords
.IP \[bu] 2
an array of values
.IP \[bu] 2
an environment variable
.IP \[bu] 2
Inherit
.PP
Some of these types are a combination of other types.
.SS String
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]([\[dq]\[aq]])[:print:]+\[rs]1\f[R]
.PP
Strings are always surrounded by double (\f[B]\[dq]\f[R]) or single
(\f[B]\[aq]\f[R], apostrophe) quotes.
Between the quotes there can be any printable character.
.PP
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
font: \[dq]Awasome 12\[dq];
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The string must be valid UTF-8, special characters can be escaped:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
text { content: \[dq]Line one\[rs]n\[rs]tIndented line two \[aq]Quoted text\[aq]\[dq]; }
text { content: \[aq]Line one\[rs]n\[rs]tIndented line two \[dq]Quoted text\[dq]\[aq]; }
text { content: \[dq]Line one\[rs]n\[rs]tIndented line two \[rs]\[dq]Quoted text\[rs]\[dq]\[dq]; }
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The following special characters can be escaped: \f[B]\[rs]b\f[R],
\f[B]\[rs]f\f[R], \f[B]\[rs]n\f[R], \f[B]\[rs]r\f[R], \f[B]\[rs]t\f[R],
\f[B]\[rs]v\f[R], \f[B]\[rs]\f[R], \f[B]\[dq]\f[R] and \f[B]\[aq]\f[R]
(double quotes inside single-quotes or in reverse don\[cq]t need
escape).
.SS Integer
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B][-+]?[:digit:]+\f[R]
.PP
An integer may contain any number.
.PP
For examples:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
lines: 12;
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Real
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B][-+]?[:digit:]+(\[rs].[:digit:]+)?\f[R]
.PP
A real is an integer with an optional fraction.
.PP
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
real: 3.4;
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The following is not valid: \f[B].3\f[R], \f[B]3.\f[R] or scientific
notation: \f[B]3.4e-3\f[R].
.SS Boolean
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B](true|false)\f[R]
.PP
Boolean value is either \f[B]true\f[R] or \f[B]false\f[R].
This is case-sensitive.
.PP
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
dynamic: false;
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Image
.PP
\f[B]rofi\f[R] support a limited set of background-image formats.
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: url(\[lq]path to image\[rq]);
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: url(\[lq]path to image\[rq], scale); where scale is: none, both,
width, height
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: linear-gradient(stop color,stop1, color, stop2 color, \&...);
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: linear-gradient(to direction, stop color,stop1, color, stop2
color, \&...); where direction is: top,left,right,bottom.
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: linear-gradient(angle, stop color,stop1, color, stop2 color,
\&...); Angle in deg,rad,grad (as used in color).
.PP
Where the \f[B]path\f[R] is a string, and \f[B]stop\f[R] color is of
type color.
.SS Color
.PP
\f[B]rofi\f[R] supports the color formats as specified in the CSS
standard (1,2,3 and some of CSS 4)
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]#{HEX}{3}\f[R] (rgb)
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]#{HEX}{4}\f[R] (rgba)
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]#{HEX}{6}\f[R] (rrggbb)
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]#{HEX}{8}\f[R] (rrggbbaa)
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]rgb[a]({INTEGER},{INTEGER},{INTEGER}[, {PERCENTAGE}])\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]rgb[a]({INTEGER}%,{INTEGER}%,{INTEGER}%[,
{PERCENTAGE}])\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]hsl[a]( {ANGLE}, {PERCENTAGE}, {PERCENTAGE} [,
{PERCENTAGE}])\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]hwb[a]( {ANGLE}, {PERCENTAGE}, {PERCENTAGE} [,
{PERCENTAGE}])\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]cmyk( {PERCENTAGE}, {PERCENTAGE}, {PERCENTAGE},
{PERCENTAGE} [, {PERCENTAGE} ])\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{named-color} [ / {PERCENTAGE} ]\f[R]
.PP
The white-space format proposed in CSS4 is also supported.
.PP
The different values are:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]{HEX}\f[R] is a hexadecimal number (`0-9a-f' case insensitive).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]{INTEGER}\f[R] value can be between 0 and 255 or 0-100 when
representing percentage.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]{ANGLE}\f[R] is the angle on the color wheel, can be in
\f[B]deg\f[R], \f[B]rad\f[R], \f[B]grad\f[R] or \f[B]turn\f[R].
When no unit is specified, degrees is assumed.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]{PERCENTAGE}\f[R] can be between 0-1.0, or 0%-100%
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]{named-color}\f[R] is one of the following colors:
.RS 2
.PP
AliceBlue, AntiqueWhite, Aqua, Aquamarine, Azure, Beige, Bisque, Black,
BlanchedAlmond, Blue, BlueViolet, Brown, BurlyWood, CadetBlue,
Chartreuse, Chocolate, Coral, CornflowerBlue, Cornsilk, Crimson, Cyan,
DarkBlue, DarkCyan, DarkGoldenRod, DarkGray, DarkGrey, DarkGreen,
DarkKhaki, DarkMagenta, DarkOliveGreen, DarkOrange, DarkOrchid, DarkRed,
DarkSalmon, DarkSeaGreen, DarkSlateBlue, DarkSlateGray, DarkSlateGrey,
DarkTurquoise, DarkViolet, DeepPink, DeepSkyBlue, DimGray, DimGrey,
DodgerBlue, FireBrick, FloralWhite, ForestGreen, Fuchsia, Gainsboro,
GhostWhite, Gold, GoldenRod, Gray, Grey, Green, GreenYellow, HoneyDew,
HotPink, IndianRed, Indigo, Ivory, Khaki, Lavender, LavenderBlush,
LawnGreen, LemonChiffon, LightBlue, LightCoral, LightCyan,
LightGoldenRodYellow, LightGray, LightGrey, LightGreen, LightPink,
LightSalmon, LightSeaGreen, LightSkyBlue, LightSlateGray,
LightSlateGrey, LightSteelBlue, LightYellow, Lime, LimeGreen, Linen,
Magenta, Maroon, MediumAquaMarine, MediumBlue, MediumOrchid,
MediumPurple, MediumSeaGreen, MediumSlateBlue, MediumSpringGreen,
MediumTurquoise, MediumVioletRed, MidnightBlue, MintCream, MistyRose,
Moccasin, NavajoWhite, Navy, OldLace, Olive, OliveDrab, Orange,
OrangeRed, Orchid, PaleGoldenRod, PaleGreen, PaleTurquoise,
PaleVioletRed, PapayaWhip, PeachPuff, Peru, Pink, Plum, PowderBlue,
Purple, RebeccaPurple, Red, RosyBrown, RoyalBlue, SaddleBrown, Salmon,
SandyBrown, SeaGreen, SeaShell, Sienna, Silver, SkyBlue, SlateBlue,
SlateGray, SlateGrey, Snow, SpringGreen, SteelBlue, Tan, Teal, Thistle,
Tomato, Turquoise, Violet, Wheat, White, WhiteSmoke, Yellow,
YellowGreen,transparent
.RE
.PP
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
background-color: #FF0000;
border-color: rgba(0,0,1, 0.5);
text-color: SeaGreen;
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
or
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
background-color: transparent;
text-color: Black;
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Text style
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B](bold|italic|underline|strikethrough|none)\f[R]
.PP
Text style indicates how the highlighted text is emphasized.
\f[B]None\f[R] indicates that no emphasis should be applied.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]bold\f[R]: make the text thicker then the surrounding text.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]italic\f[R]: put the highlighted text in script type (slanted).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]underline\f[R]: put a line under the text.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]strikethrough\f[R]: put a line through the text.
.PP
The following options are available on pango 1.50.0 and up:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]uppercase\f[R]: Uppercase the text.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]lowercase\f[R]: Lowercase the text.
.PP
The following option is disabled as pango crashes on this if there is
eel upsizing or wrapping.
This will be re-enabled once fixed:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]capitalize\f[R]: Capitalize the text.
.SS Line style
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B](dash|solid)\f[R]
.PP
Indicates how a line should be drawn.
It currently supports:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]dash\f[R]: a dashed line, where the gap is the same width as the
dash
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]solid\f[R]: a solid line
.SS Distance
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Integer}px\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Real}em\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Real}ch\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Real}%\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Real}mm\f[R]
.PP
A distance can be specified in 3 different units:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]px\f[R]: Screen pixels.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]em\f[R]: Relative to text height.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]ch\f[R]: Relative to width of a single number.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]mm\f[R]: Actual size in millimeters (based on dpi).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]%\f[R]: Percentage of the \f[B]monitor\f[R] size.
.PP
Distances used in the horizontal direction use the monitor width.
Distances in the vertical direction use the monitor height.
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
   padding: 10%;
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
On a full-HD (1920x1080) monitor, it defines a padding of 192 pixels on
the left and right side and 108 pixels on the top and bottom.
.SS Calculating sizes
.PP
Rofi supports some maths in calculating sizes.
For this it uses the CSS syntax:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
width: calc( 100% - 37px );
\f[R]
.fi
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
width: calc( 20% min 512 );
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
It supports the following operations:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]+\f[R] : Add
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]-\f[R] : Subtract
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]/\f[R] : Divide
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]-\f[R] : Multiply
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]modulo\f[R] : Modulo
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]min\f[R] : Minimum of lvalue or rvalue;
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]max\f[R] : Maximum of lvalue or rvalue;
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]floor\f[R] : Round down lvalue to the next multiple of rvalue
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]ceil\f[R] : Round up lvalue to the next multiple of rvalue
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]round\f[R] : Round lvalue to the next multiple of rvalue
.PP
It uses the C precedence ordering.
.SS Padding
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Integer}\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Distance}\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Distance} {Distance}\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Distance} {Distance} {Distance}\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Distance} {Distance} {Distance} {Distance}\f[R]
.PP
If no unit is specified, pixels are assumed.
.PP
The different number of fields in the formats are parsed like:
.IP \[bu] 2
1 field: \f[B]all\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
2 fields: \f[B]top&bottom\f[R] \f[B]left&right\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
3 fields: \f[B]top\f[R], \f[B]left&right\f[R], \f[B]bottom\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
4 fields: \f[B]top\f[R], \f[B]right\f[R], \f[B]bottom\f[R],
\f[B]left\f[R]
.SS Border
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Integer}\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Distance}\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Distance} {Distance}\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Distance} {Distance} {Distance}\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Distance} {Distance} {Distance} {Distance}\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Distance} {Line style}\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Distance} {Line style} {Distance} {Line style}\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Distance} {Line style} {Distance} {Line style} {Distance}
{Line style}\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]{Distance} {Line style} {Distance} {Line style} {Distance}
{Line style} {Distance} {Line style}\f[R]
.PP
Borders are identical to padding, except that each distance field has a
line style property.
.RS
.PP
When no unit is specified, pixels are assumed.
.RE
.SS Position
.PP
Indicate a place on the window/monitor.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
┌─────────────┬─────────────┬─────────────┐
│ north west  │    north    │  north east │
├─────────────┼─────────────┼─────────────┤
│   west      │   center    │     east    │
├─────────────┼─────────────┼─────────────┤
│ south west  │    south    │  south east │
└─────────────┴─────────────┴─────────────┘
\f[R]
.fi
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B](center|east|north|west|south|north east|north west|south
west|south east)\f[R]
.SS Visibility
.PP
It is possible to hide widgets:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
inputbar {
    enabled: false;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Reference
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]\[at]{PROPERTY NAME}\f[R]
.PP
A reference can point to another reference.
Currently, the maximum number of redirects is 20.
A property always refers to another property.
It cannot be used for a subpart of the property.
For example, this is not valid:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
highlight: bold \[at]pink;
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
But this is:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
* {
    myhigh: bold #FAA;
}

window {
    highlight: \[at]myhigh;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]var(PROPERTY NAME, DEFAULT)\f[R]
.PP
A reference can point to another reference.
Currently, the maximum number of redirects is 20.
A property always refers to another property.
It cannot be used for a subpart of the property.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
window {
    width: var( width, 30%);
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If the property \f[B]width\f[R] is set globally (\f[B]*{}\f[R]) that
value is used, if the property \f[B]width\f[R] is not set, the default
value is used.
.SS Orientation
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B](horizontal|vertical)\f[R]
.PP
Specify the orientation of the widget.
.SS Cursor
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B](default|pointer|text)\f[R]
.PP
Specify the type of mouse cursor that is set when the mouse pointer is
over the widget.
.SS List of keywords
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B][ keyword, keyword ]\f[R]
.PP
A list starts with a `[\[cq] and ends with a \[cq]]'.
The entries in the list are comma-separated.
The \f[B]keyword\f[R] in the list refers to an widget name.
.SS List of values
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B][ value, value, ...
]\f[R]
.PP
An list starts with a `[\[cq] and ends with a \[cq]]'.
The entries in the list are comma-separated.
.SS Environment variable
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]${:alnum:}\f[R]
.PP
This will parse the environment variable as the property value.
(that then can be any of the above types).
The environment variable should be an alphanumeric string without
white-space.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
* {
    background-color: ${BG};
}
\f[R]
.fi
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]env(ENVIRONMENT, default)\f[R]
.PP
This will parse the environment variable as the property value.
(that then can be any of the above types).
The environment variable should be an alphanumeric string without
white-space.
If the environment value is not found, the default value is used.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
window {
    width: env(WIDTH, 40%);
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If environment WIDTH is set, then that value is parsed, otherwise the
default value (\f[B]40%\f[R]).
.SS Inherit
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[B]inherit\f[R]
.PP
Inherits the property from its parent widget.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
mainbox {
    border-color: inherit;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.SH Elements paths
.PP
Element paths exists of two parts, the first part refers to the actual
widget by name.
Some widgets have an extra state.
.PP
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
element selected {
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Here \f[B]element selected\f[R] is the name of the widget,
\f[B]selected\f[R] is the state of the widget.
.PP
The difference between dots and spaces is purely cosmetic.
These are all the same:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
element .selected {

element.selected {
}
element selected {
}
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Supported element paths
.SS Base widgets
.PP
The default widgets available in \f[B]rofi\f[R] and the default
hierarchic:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]window\f[R]
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]overlay\f[R]: the overlay widget.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]mainbox\f[R]: The mainbox box.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]inputbar\f[R]: The input bar box.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]box\f[R]: the horizontal \[at]box packing the widgets
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]case-indicator\f[R]: the case/sort indicator \[at]textbox
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]prompt\f[R]: the prompt \[at]textbox
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]entry\f[R]: the main entry \[at]textbox
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]num-rows\f[R]: Shows the total number of rows.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]num-filtered-rows\f[R]: Shows the total number of rows after
filtering.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]textbox-current-entry\f[R]: Shows the text of the currently
selected entry.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]icon-current-entry\f[R]: Shows the icon of the currently selected
entry.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]listview\f[R]: The listview.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]scrollbar\f[R]: the listview scrollbar
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]element\f[R]: a box in the listview holding the entries
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]element-icon\f[R]: the widget in the listview\[cq]s entry showing
the (optional) icon
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]element-index\f[R]: the widget in the listview\[cq]s entry
keybindable index (1,2,3..0)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]element-text\f[R]: the widget in the listview\[cq]s entry showing
the text.
.RE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]mode-switcher\f[R]: the main horizontal \[at]box packing the
buttons.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]button\f[R]: the buttons \[at]textbox for each mode
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]message\f[R]: The container holding the textbox.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]textbox\f[R]: the message textbox
.RE
.RE
.PP
Note that these path names match the default theme.
Themes that provide a custom layout will have different elements, and
structure.
.SS State
.PP
State: State of widget
.PP
Optional flag(s) indicating state of the widget, used for theming.
.PP
These are appended after the name or class of the widget.
.SS Example
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
button selected.normal { }

element selected.urgent { }
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Currently only the entrybox and scrollbar have states:
.SS Entrybox
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
{visible modifier}.{state}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Where \f[B]visible modifier\f[R] can be:
.IP \[bu] 2
normal: no modification
.IP \[bu] 2
selected: the entry is selected/highlighted by user
.IP \[bu] 2
alternate: the entry is at an alternating row (uneven row)
.PP
Where \f[B]state\f[R] is:
.IP \[bu] 2
normal: no modification
.IP \[bu] 2
urgent: this entry is marked urgent
.IP \[bu] 2
active: this entry is marked active
.PP
These can be mixed.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
nametotextbox selected.active {
    background-color: #003642;
    text-color: #008ed4;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Sets all selected textboxes marked active to the given text and
background color.
Note that a state modifies the original element, it therefore contains
all the properties of that element.
.SS Scrollbar
.PP
The scrollbar uses the \f[B]handle\f[R] state when drawing the small
scrollbar handle.
This allows the colors used for drawing the handle to be set
independently.
.SH Widget properties
.PP
The following properties are currently supported:
.SS all widgets
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]enabled\f[R]: enable/disable rendering of the widget
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]padding\f[R]: padding Padding on the inside of the widget
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]margin\f[R]: padding Margin on the outside of the widget
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]border\f[R]: border Border around the widget (between padding and
margin)/
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]border-radius\f[R]: padding Sets a radius on the corners of the
borders.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]background-color\f[R]: color Background color
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]background-image\f[R]: image Background image
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]border-color\f[R]: color Color of the border
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]cursor\f[R]: cursor Type of mouse cursor that is set when the mouse
pointer is hovered over the widget.
.SS window
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]font\f[R]: string The font used in the window
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]transparency\f[R]: string Indicating if transparency should be used
and what type:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]real\f[R] - True transparency.
Only works with a compositor.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]background\f[R] - Take a screenshot of the background image and use
that.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]screenshot\f[R] - Take a screenshot of the screen and use that.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Path\f[R] to png file - Use an image.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]location\f[R]: position The place of the anchor on the monitor
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]anchor\f[R]: anchor The anchor position on the window
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]fullscreen\f[R]: boolean Window is fullscreen.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]width\f[R]: distance The width of the window
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]x-offset\f[R]: distance
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]y-offset\f[R]: distance The offset of the window to the anchor
point, allowing you to push the window left/right/up/down
.SS scrollbar Properties
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]background-color\f[R]: color
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]handle-width\f[R]: distance
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]handle-color\f[R]: color
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]border-color\f[R]: color
.SS box
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]orientation\f[R]: orientation Set the direction the elements are
packed.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]spacing\f[R]: distance Distance between the packed elements.
.SS textbox
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]background-color\f[R]: color
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]border-color\f[R]: the color used for the border around the widget.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]font\f[R]: the font used by this textbox (string).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]str\f[R]/\f[B]content\f[R]: the string to display by this textbox
(string).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]vertical-align\f[R]: Vertical alignment of the text.
A number between 0 (top) and 1 (bottom).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]horizontal-align\f[R]: Horizontal alignment of the text.
A number between 0 (left) and 1 (right).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]text-color\f[R]: the text color to use.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]text-transform\f[R]: text style {color} for the whole text.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]highlight\f[R]: text style {color}.
color is optional, multiple highlight styles can be added like: bold
underline italic #000000; This option is only available on the
\f[B]element-text\f[R] widget.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]width\f[R]: override the desired width for the textbox.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]content\f[R]: Set the displayed text (String).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]placeholder\f[R]: Set the displayed text (String) when nothing is
entered.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]placeholder-markup\f[R]: If true, placeholder text supports pango
markup for stylizing.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]placeholder-color\f[R]: Color of the placeholder text.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]blink\f[R]: Enable/Disable blinking on an input textbox (Boolean).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]markup\f[R]: Force markup on, beware that only valid pango markup
strings are shown.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]tab-stops\f[R]: array of distances.
Set the location of tab stops by their distance from the beginning of
the line.
Each distance should be greater than the previous one.
The text appears to the right of the tab stop position (other alignments
are not supported yet).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]cursor-width\f[R]: The width of the cursor.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]cursor-color\f[R]: The color used to draw the cursor.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]cursor-outline\f[R]: Enable a border (outline) around the cursor.
(Boolean)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]cursor-outline-width\f[R]: The width of the border around the
cursor.
(Double)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]cursor-outline-color\f[R]: The color to use for the cursor outline.
(Color)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]text-outline\f[R]: Enable a border (outline) around the text.
(Boolean)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]text-outline-width\f[R]: The width of the border around the text.
(Double)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]text-outline-color\f[R]: The color to use for the text outline.
(Color)
.SS listview
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]columns\f[R]: integer Number of columns to show (at least 1)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]fixed-height\f[R]: boolean Always show \f[B]lines\f[R] rows, even
if fewer elements are available.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]dynamic\f[R]: boolean \f[B]True\f[R] if the size should change when
filtering the list, \f[B]False\f[R] if it should keep the original
height.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]scrollbar\f[R]: boolean If the scrollbar should be
enabled/disabled.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]scrollbar-width\f[R]: distance Width of the scrollbar
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]cycle\f[R]: boolean When navigating, it should wrap around
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]spacing\f[R]: distance Spacing between the elements (both vertical
and horizontal)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]lines\f[R]: integer Number of rows to show in the list view.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]layout\f[R]: orientation Indicate how elements are stacked.
Horizontal implements the dmenu style.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]reverse\f[R]: boolean Reverse the ordering (top down to bottom up).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]flow\f[R]: orientation The order the elements are layed out.
Vertical is the original `column' view.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]fixed-columns\f[R]: boolean Do not reduce the number of columns
shown when number of visible elements is not enough to fill them all.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]require-input\f[R]: boolean Listview requires user input to be
unhidden.
The list is still present and hitting accept will activate the first
entry.
.SH Listview widget
.PP
The listview widget is special container widget.
It has the following fixed children widgets:
.IP \[bu] 2
0 or more \f[B]element\f[R] widgets of the type box.
.IP \[bu] 2
An optional \f[B]scrollbar\f[R] widget.
This can be enabled using the scrollbar property.
.PP
These cannot be changed using the \f[B]children\f[R] property.
.PP
Each Entry displayed by listview is captured by a \f[B]box\f[R] called
\f[B]element\f[R].
An \f[B]element\f[R] widget can contain the following special child
widgets:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]element-icon\f[R]: An icon widget showing the icon associated to
the entry.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]element-text\f[R]: A textbox widget showing the text associated to
the entry.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]element-index\f[R]: A textbox widget that shows the shortcut
keybinding number.
.PP
By default the \f[B]element-icon\f[R] and \f[B]element-text\f[R] child
widgets are added to the \f[B]element\f[R].
This can be modified using the \f[B]children\f[R] property or the
\f[B][no]-show-icons\f[R] option.
.PP
A child added with another name is treated the same as the special
widget described in the advanced layout section.
.SS listview text highlight
.PP
The \f[B]element-text\f[R] widget in the \f[B]listview\f[R] is the one
used to show the text.
On this widget set the \f[B]highlight\f[R] property (only place this
property is used) to change the style of highlighting.
The \f[B]highlight\f[R] property consist of the \f[B]text-style\f[R]
property and a color.
.PP
To disable highlighting:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
  element-text {
    highlight: None;
  }
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To set to red underlined:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
  element-text {
    highlight: underline red;
  }
\f[R]
.fi
.SH Layout
.PP
The new format allows the layout of the \f[B]rofi\f[R] window to be
tweaked extensively.
For each widget, the themer can specify padding, margin, border, font,
and more.
It even allows, as an advanced feature, to pack widgets in a custom
structure.
.SS Basic layout structure
.PP
The whole view is made out of boxes that pack other boxes or widgets.
The box can be vertical or horizontal.
This is loosely inspired by GTK (http://gtk.org/).
.PP
The current layout of \f[B]rofi\f[R] is structured as follows:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ window {BOX:vertical}                                                              │
│ ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│ │ mainbox  {BOX:vertical}                                                       │  │
│ │ ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │  │
│ │ │ inputbar {BOX:horizontal}                                                 │ │  │
│ │ │ ┌─────────┐ ┌─┐ ┌───────────────────────────────┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐ │ │  │
│ │ │ │ prompt  │ │:│ │ entry                         │ │#fr│ │ / │ │#ns│ │ci │ │ │  │
│ │ │ └─────────┘ └─┘ └───────────────────────────────┘ └───┘ └───┘ └───┘ └───┘ │ │  │
│ │ └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │  │
│ │                                                                               │  │
│ │ ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │  │
│ │ │ message                                                                   │ │  │
│ │ │ ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ │  │
│ │ │ │ textbox                                                               │ │ │  │
│ │ │ └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │ │  │
│ │ └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │  │
│ │                                                                               │  │
│ │ ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │  │
│ │ │ listview                                                                  │ │  │
│ │ │ ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │ │  │
│ │ │ │ element                                                             │   │ │  │
│ │ │ │ ┌─────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │   │ │  │
│ │ │ │ │element─icon     │ │element─text                                 │ │   │ │  │
│ │ │ │ └─────────────────┘ └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │   │ │  │
│ │ │ └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │ │  │
│ │ └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │  │
│ │                                                                               │  │
│ │ ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │  │
│ │ │  mode─switcher {BOX:horizontal}                                           │ │  │
│ │ │ ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐ ┌───────────────┐ │ │  │
│ │ │ │ Button        │   │ Button        │  │ Button       │ │ Button        │ │ │  │
│ │ │ └───────────────┘   └───────────────┘  └──────────────┘ └───────────────┘ │ │  │
│ │ └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │  │
│ └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

\f[R]
.fi
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
ci is the case-indicator
.IP \[bu] 2
fr is the num-filtered-rows
.IP \[bu] 2
ns is the num-rows
.RE
.SS Error message structure
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ window {BOX:vertical}                                                            │
│ ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│ │ error─message {BOX:vertical}                                                │  │
│ │ ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │  │
│ │ │ textbox                                                                │  │  │
│ │ └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │  │
│ └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Advanced layout
.PP
The layout of \f[B]rofi\f[R] can be tweaked by packing the `fixed'
widgets in a custom structure.
.PP
The following widgets are fixed, as they provide core \f[B]rofi\f[R]
functionality:
.IP \[bu] 2
prompt
.IP \[bu] 2
entry
.IP \[bu] 2
overlay
.IP \[bu] 2
case-indicator
.IP \[bu] 2
message
.IP \[bu] 2
listview
.IP \[bu] 2
mode-switcher
.IP \[bu] 2
num-rows
.IP \[bu] 2
num-filtered-rows
.PP
The following keywords are defined and can be used to automatically pack
a subset of the widgets.
These are used in the default theme as depicted in the figure above.
.IP \[bu] 2
mainbox Packs: \f[B]inputbar, message, listview, mode-switcher\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
inputbar Packs: \f[B]prompt,entry,case-indicator\f[R]
.PP
Any widget name starting with \f[B]textbox\f[R] is a textbox widget,
others are box widgets and can pack other widgets.
.PP
There are several special widgets that can be used by prefixing the name
of the widget:
.SS Textbox widget
.PP
This is a read-only textbox widget.
The displayed string can be set with \f[B]content\f[R].
.PP
Example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
textbox-custom {
  expand: false;
  content: \[dq]My Message\[dq];
}
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Icon
.PP
This is an icon widget.
The displayed icon can be set with \f[B]filename\f[R] and size with
\f[B]size\f[R].
If the property \f[B]action\f[R] is set, it acts as a button.
\f[B]action\f[R] can be set to a keybinding name and completes that
action.
(see rofi -show keys for a list).
.PP
If the \f[B]squared\f[R] property is set to \f[B]false\f[R] the widget
height and width are not forced to be equal.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
icon-paste {
    expand: false;
    filename: \[dq]gtk-paste\[dq];
    size: 24;
    vertical-align: 0.5;
    action: \[dq]kb-primary-paste\[dq];
}
\f[R]
.fi
.SS button
.PP
This is a textbox widget that can have a `clickable' action.
The \f[B]action\f[R] can be set to: \f[B]keybinding\f[R]: accepts a
keybinding name and completes that action.
(see rofi -show keys for a list).
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
button-paste {
    expand: false;
    content: \[dq]My Clickable Message\[dq];
    vertical-align: 0.5;
    action: \[dq]kb-primary-paste\[dq];
}
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Children
.PP
To specify children, set the \f[B]children\f[R] property (this always
happens on the \f[B]box\f[R] child, see example below):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
inputbar {
  children: [prompt,entry,overlay,case-indicator];
}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The theme needs to be updated to match the hierarchy specified.
.PP
Below is an example of a theme emulating dmenu:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
* {
    background-color:      Black;
    text-color:            White;
    border-color:          White;
    font:            \[dq]Times New Roman 12\[dq];
}

window {
    anchor:     north;
    location:   north;
    width:      100%;
    padding:    4px;
    children:   [ horibox ];
}

horibox {
    orientation: horizontal;
    children:   [ prompt, entry, listview ];
}

listview {
    layout:     horizontal;
    spacing:    5px;
    lines:      10;
}

entry {
    expand:     false;
    width:      10em;
}

element {
    padding: 0px 2px;
}
element selected {
    background-color: SteelBlue;
}
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Padding and margin
.PP
Just like CSS, \f[B]rofi\f[R] uses the box model for each widget.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ margin                                                           │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │ border                                                     │  │
│  │ ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │  │
│  │ │ padding                                                │ │  │
│  │ │ ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ │  │
│  │ │ │ content                                            │ │ │  │
│  │ │ └────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │ │  │
│  │ └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │  │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Explanation of the different parts:
.IP \[bu] 2
Content - The content of the widget.
.IP \[bu] 2
Padding - Clears an area around the widget.
The padding shows the background color of the widget.
.IP \[bu] 2
Border - A border that goes around the padding and content.
The border use the border-color of the widget.
.IP \[bu] 2
Margin - Clears an area outside the border.
The margin is transparent.
.PP
The box model allows us to add a border around elements, and to define
space between elements.
.PP
The size of each margin, border, and padding can be set.
For the border, a linestyle and radius can be set.
.SS Spacing
.PP
Widgets that can pack more then one child widget (currently box and
listview) have the \f[B]spacing\f[R] property.
This property sets the distance between the packed widgets (both
horizontally and vertically).
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ ┌────────┐ s ┌────────┐ s ┌────────┐  │
│ │ child  │ p │ child  │ p │ child  │  │
│ │        │ a │        │ a │        │  │
│ │        │ c │        │ c │        │  │
│ │        │ i │        │ i │        │  │
│ │        │ n │        │ n │        │  │
│ └────────┘ g └────────┘ g └────────┘  │
└───────────────────────────────────────┘
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Advanced box packing
.PP
More dynamic spacing can be achieved by adding dummy widgets, for
example to make one widget centered:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ┌───────────────┐  ┌────────┐  ┌───────────────┐  │
│  │ dummy         │  │ child  │  │ dummy         │  │
│  │ expand: true; │  │        │  │ expand: true; │  │
│  │               │  │        │  │               │  │
│  │               │  │        │  │               │  │
│  │               │  │        │  │               │  │
│  └───────────────┘  └────────┘  └───────────────┘  │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If both dummy widgets are set to expand, \f[B]child\f[R] will be
centered.
Depending on the \f[B]expand\f[R] flag of child the remaining space will
be equally divided between both dummy and child widget (expand enabled),
or both dummy widgets (expand disabled).
.SH Debugging
.PP
To get debug information from the parser, run rofi like:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=Parser rofi -show run
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Syntax errors are shown in a popup and printed out to command line with
the above command.
.PP
To see the elements queried during running, run:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=Theme rofi -show run
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To test minor changes, part of the theme can be passed on the command
line, for example to set it to full-screen:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
rofi -theme-str \[aq]window { fullscreen:true;}\[aq] -show run
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Another syntax to modify theme properties is:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
rofi -theme+window+fullscreen true -show run
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To print the current theme, run:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
rofi -dump-theme
\f[R]
.fi
.SH Media support
.PP
Parts of the theme can be conditionally loaded, like the CSS
\f[B]\[at]media\f[R] option.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
\[at]media ( min-width: 120 ) {

}
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
It supports the following keys as constraint:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]min-width\f[R]: load when width is bigger or equal then value.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]max-width\f[R]: load when width is smaller then value.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]min-height\f[R]: load when height is bigger or equal then value.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]max-height\f[R]: load when height is smaller then value.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]min-aspect-ratio\f[R] load when aspect ratio is over value.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]max-aspect-ratio\f[R]: load when aspect ratio is under value.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]monitor-id\f[R]: The monitor id, see rofi -help for id\[cq]s.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]enabled\f[R]: Boolean option to enable.
Supports environment variable or DMENU to detect if in dmenu mode.
.PP
\[at]media takes an integer number or a fraction, for integer number
\f[B]px\f[R] can be added.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
\[at]media ( min-width: 120 px ) {

}
\f[R]
.fi
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
\[at]media ( enabled: env(DO_LIGHT, false )) {

}
\f[R]
.fi
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
\[at]media ( enabled: DMENU) {

}
\f[R]
.fi
.SH Conflicting constraints
.PP
It is possible to define conflicting constraints in the theme.
These conflicts are not explicitly reported.
The most common example is forcing a specific window size, for example
by enabling full-screen mode, having number of lines set in the listview
and having the listview expand to available space.
There is clearly a conflict in these 3 constraints.
In this case, listview will not limit to the number of lines, but tries
to fill the available space.
It is up to the theme designer to make sure the theme handles this
correctly.
.SH Font Parsing
.PP
Rofi uses pango (https://pango.gnome.org/) for font rendering.
The font should be specified in a format that pango understands.
This normally is the font name followed by the font size.
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
mono 18
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Or
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
FontAwesome 22
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
From the pango manpage:
.PP
The string must have the form
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
\[rs][FAMILY-LIST] \[rs][STYLE-OPTIONS] \[rs][SIZE] \[rs][VARIATIONS]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
where FAMILY-LIST is a comma-separated list of families optionally
terminated by a comma, STYLE_OPTIONS is a whitespace-separated list of
words where each word describes one of style, variant, weight, stretch,
or gravity, and SIZE is a decimal number (size in points) or optionally
followed by the unit modifier \[lq]px\[rq] for absolute size.
VARIATIONS is a comma-separated list of font variation specifications of
the form \[lq]\f[B]axis\f[R]=value\[rq] (the = sign is optional).
.PP
The following words are understood as styles: \[lq]Normal\[rq],
\[lq]Roman\[rq], \[lq]Oblique\[rq], \[lq]Italic\[rq].
.PP
The following words are understood as variants: \[lq]Small-Caps\[rq],
\[lq]All-Small-Caps\[rq], \[lq]Petite-Caps\[rq],
\[lq]All-Petite-Caps\[rq], \[lq]Unicase\[rq], \[lq]Title-Caps\[rq].
.PP
The following words are understood as weights: \[lq]Thin\[rq],
\[lq]Ultra-Light\[rq], \[lq]Extra-Light\[rq], \[lq]Light\[rq],
\[lq]Semi-Light\[rq], \[lq]Demi-Light\[rq], \[lq]Book\[rq],
\[lq]Regular\[rq], \[lq]Medium\[rq], \[lq]Semi-Bold\[rq],
\[lq]Demi-Bold\[rq], \[lq]Bold\[rq], \[lq]Ultra-Bold\[rq],
\[lq]Extra-Bold\[rq], \[lq]Heavy\[rq], \[lq]Black\[rq],
\[lq]Ultra-Black\[rq], \[lq]Extra-Black\[rq].
.PP
The following words are understood as stretch values:
\[lq]Ultra-Condensed\[rq], \[lq]Extra-Condensed\[rq],
\[lq]Condensed\[rq], \[lq]Semi-Condensed\[rq], \[lq]Semi-Expanded\[rq],
\[lq]Expanded\[rq], \[lq]Extra-Expanded\[rq], \[lq]Ultra-Expanded\[rq].
.PP
The following words are understood as gravity values:
\[lq]Not-Rotated\[rq], \[lq]South\[rq], \[lq]Upside-Down\[rq],
\[lq]North\[rq], \[lq]Rotated-Left\[rq], \[lq]East\[rq],
\[lq]Rotated-Right\[rq], \[lq]West\[rq].
.PP
Any one of the options may be absent.
If FAMILY-LIST is absent, then the family_name field of the resulting
font description will be initialized to NULL.
If STYLE-OPTIONS is missing, then all style options will be set to the
default values.
If SIZE is missing, the size in the resulting font description will be
set to 0.
.PP
A typical example:
.PP
\[lq]Cantarell Italic Light 15 \[ga]wght\[ga]=200\[rq]
.SH Icon Handling
.PP
Rofi supports 3 ways of specifying an icon:
.IP \[bu] 2
Filename
.IP \[bu] 2
icon-name, this is looked up via the icon-theme.
.IP \[bu] 2
Markup String.
It renders a string as an icon.
.PP
For the first two options, GdkPixbuf is used to open and render the
icons.
This in general gives support for most required image formats.
For the string option it uses Pango to render the string.
The string needs to start with a \f[B]<span\f[R] tag, that allows you to
set color and font.
.PP
Markup string:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
echo -en \[dq]testing\[rs]0icon\[rs]x1f<span color=\[aq]red\[aq]>⏻</span>\[dq] | ./rofi -dmenu
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Getting supported icon formats:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=Helpers.IconFetcher rofi
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
This uses the debug framework and prints out a list of supported image
file extensions.
.SH Multiple file handling
.PP
The rasi file format offers two methods of including other files.
This can be used to modify existing themes, or have multiple variations
on a theme.
.IP \[bu] 2
import: Import and parse a second file.
.IP \[bu] 2
theme: Discard theme, and load file as a fresh theme.
.PP
Syntax:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
\[at]import \[dq]myfile\[dq]
\[at]theme \[dq]mytheme\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The specified file can either by \f[I]name\f[R],
\f[I]filename\f[R],\f[I]full path\f[R].
.PP
If a filename is provided, it will try to resolve it in the following
order:
.IP \[bu] 2
If path is absolute and file exists, it will open the file.
This includes expansion of `\[ti]' or `\[ti]user'
.IP \[bu] 2
On an \f[B]\[at]import\f[R] or \f[B]\[at]theme\f[R] it looks in the
directory of the file that tried to include it.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]${XDG_CONFIG_HOME}/rofi/themes/\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]${XDG_CONFIG_HOME}/rofi/\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]${XDG_DATA_HOME}/rofi/themes/\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]${INSTALL PREFIX}/share/rofi/themes/\f[R]
.PP
A name is resolved (if it has no valid extension) as a filename by
appending the \f[B].rasi\f[R] and the \f[B].rasinc\f[R] extension.
It will first look for files with \f[B].rasi\f[R], then for files with
\f[B].rasinc\f[R].
.SH Examples
.PP
Several examples are installed together with \f[B]rofi\f[R].
These can be found in \f[B]{datadir}/rofi/themes/\f[R], where
\f[B]{datadir}\f[R] is the install path of \f[B]rofi\f[R] data.
When installed using a package manager, this is usually:
\f[B]/usr/share/\f[R].
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
rofi(1), rofi-script(5), rofi-theme-selector(1)
